    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The performance characteristics of single injection-extraction doublets in uniform flow fields are studied. Attention is given mainly to limiting stream lines, which form barriers in these fields. The design of such barriers for specific purposes such as the prevention of sea water intrusion into aquifers is investigated. Improved techniques to interpret and evaluate the results are developed. Consideration is given to: (1) the relative strength of the flow field with respect to injection well and production well rates, (2) the angle of orientation of a doublet with respect to the direction of flow in the uniform field, (3) travel times along stream lines within a doublet field, and (4) the formation of a hydraulic barrier with respect to different hydrodynamic characteristics of an aquifer.Type curves are constructed to analyze the interflow between single well pairs in regional flow fields. For a doublet in an aquifer with a regional flow field, four methods are presented for graphing tracer-material concentration in the extraction well as a function of time. Theoretical and practical aspects of the shielding characteristics of injection-extraction doublets in uniform regional flow fields are examined. The study then is extended to multiple well systems composed of arrays of doublets, with and without regional flow. Also, a mathematical model is developed to calculate and graph the stream lines, isopotentials, and iso-time interfaces (fronts). The variables examined are similar to those of the foregoing single doublet. an exact analytical solution is developed to track fronts formed in isolated doublet systems.Necessary theory and computer software programs are developed to apply the basic doublet-regional flow concept to an 18-well system (9 injectors and 9 extractors) proposed for operation along the Palo Alto Bay Front area as a barrier to sea water intrusion into a ground water aquifer. The shielding characteristics of these doublets in regional flow fields with different angles of orientation are analyzed. The results indicate how barrier formation can be optimized.",
